 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Glycophorin A is depicted in the membrane diagram as —", "opts": ["An enzyme of the HMP shunt", "A splenic macrophage receptor", "A cytoskeletal filament under the bilayer", "A protein spanning the lipid bilayer"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Glycophorin A is the second labelled protein crossing the lipid bilayer. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-019"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Which structure is labelled at the junctional complex together with Band 4.1 in the diagram?", "opts": ["Actin", "Ankyrin", "Band 3", "ConA"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Actin and Band 4.1 are labelled at the junctional complex of the spectrin lattice. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-020"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Odd one out (proteins forming the submembrane cytoskeleton in the diagram):", "opts": ["Band 4.1 protein", "Glycophorin A", "Spectrin tetramer", "Actin filaments"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Glycophorin A is a transmembrane protein; the other three form the underlying skeleton. (Book p241)", "type": "odd", "id": "PEDS-C51-021"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "True or False — In hereditary spherocytosis the red cell loses its biconcave shape because of a defect in the globin chain.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 1, "exp": "False — the defect is in RBC cytoskeletal proteins; globin chain defects cause haemoglobinopathies. (Book p241)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-022"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "True or False — Splenomegaly in hereditary spherocytosis is the consequence of red cells being destroyed in the splenic microcirculation.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 0, "exp": "True — entrapment in the splenic microcirculation → haemolysis → splenomegaly. (Book p241)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-023"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Which inheritance pattern best fits this family history?", "opts": ["Autosomal dominant", "X-linked recessive", "Autosomal recessive", "Sporadic somatic mutation"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Hereditary spherocytosis is autosomal dominant, so an affected parent is typical. (Book p241)", "type": "case", "stem": "A 7-year-old boy has intermittent jaundice and a firm spleen 4 cm below the costal margin; his father had his spleen removed in childhood for anaemia.", "id": "PEDS-C51-024"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — pathogenesis · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The membrane lipid layer that the labelled proteins traverse in the diagram is the —", "opts": ["Glycocalyx", "Lipid bilayer", "Sarcolemma", "Basement membrane"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The diagram labels the lipid bilayer through which Band 3 and Glycophorin A pass. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-025"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The onset of anaemia and jaundice in hereditary spherocytosis is best described as —", "opts": ["Always at birth", "Always after 6 months of age", "Variable", "Only after adolescence"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The book describes a variable onset of anaemia and jaundice. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-026"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Anaemia and jaundice in hereditary spherocytosis are due to ___ of RBCs.", "opts": ["Impaired production", "Ineffective erythropoiesis", "Sequestration without destruction", "Increased lysis"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Variable onset of anaemia and jaundice d/t ↑ lysis of RBCs. (Book p241)", "type": "fill", "id": "PEDS-C51-027"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "What proportion of children with hereditary spherocytosis present with neonatal jaundice?", "opts": ["50%", "90%", "10%", "25%"], "ans": 0, "exp": "50% of patients present as neonatal jaundice. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-028"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Which examination finding is expected in a child with hereditary spherocytosis?", "opts": ["Generalised lymphadenopathy", "Splenomegaly", "Absent thumbs", "Café-au-lait macules"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Splenomegaly is listed among the clinical features. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-029"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The type of gallstone characteristically formed in hereditary spherocytosis is —", "opts": ["Struvite stone", "Cholesterol stone", "Pigment stone", "Mixed stone"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Pigment gallstones form because of increased bilirubin production. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-030"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Pigment gallstones develop in hereditary spherocytosis because of increased ___ production.", "opts": ["Lactate dehydrogenase", "Urobilinogen", "Haptoglobin", "Bilirubin"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Pigment gallstones — ↑ bilirubin production. (Book p241)", "type": "fill", "id": "PEDS-C51-031"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Which feature of hereditary spherocytosis is described as an acute presentation?", "opts": ["Aplastic crisis", "Positive family history", "Pigment gallstones", "Splenomegaly"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Aplastic crisis is listed as the acute presentation. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-032"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The organism that precipitates aplastic crisis in hereditary spherocytosis is —", "opts": ["Cytomegalovirus", "Parvovirus B19", "Epstein-Barr virus", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Parvovirus B19 infection in a patient with hereditary spherocytosis causes the aplastic crisis. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-033"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Parvovirus B19 infection in hereditary spherocytosis produces pancytopenia by causing —", "opts": ["Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Splenic sequestration", "Bone marrow dysfunction", "Autoimmune haemolysis"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Parvovirus B19 → bone marrow dysfunction → pancytopenia. (Book p241)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-034"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The haematological picture produced by parvovirus B19 infection in hereditary spherocytosis is ___.", "opts": ["Leukaemoid reaction", "Eosinophilia", "Isolated thrombocytosis", "Pancytopenia"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Bone marrow dysfunction from parvovirus B19 → pancytopenia. (Book p241)", "type": "fill", "id": "PEDS-C51-035"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Match each clinical feature of hereditary spherocytosis with its stated mechanism/qualifier:", "opts": ["A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1", "A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1", "A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4", "A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2"], "ans": 0, "exp": "These are the qualifiers the book attaches to each feature. Correct pairs: Anaemia and jaundice→Increased lysis of RBCs; Neonatal jaundice→Seen in 50% of patients; Pigment gallstones→Increased bilirubin production; Aplastic crisis→Parvovirus B19 causing marrow dysfunction. (Book p241)", "type": "match", "pairs": [["Anaemia and jaundice", "Parvovirus B19 causing marrow dysfunction"], ["Neonatal jaundice", "Increased lysis of RBCs"], ["Pigment gallstones", "Seen in 50% of patients"], ["Aplastic crisis", "Increased bilirubin production"]], "id": "PEDS-C51-036"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "True or False — A positive family history is a recognised clinical pointer in hereditary spherocytosis.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 0, "exp": "True — 'Family history +' is listed among the clinical features, in keeping with autosomal dominant inheritance. (Book p241)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-037"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "True or False — Aplastic crisis in hereditary spherocytosis presents with an isolated fall in the platelet count only.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 1, "exp": "False — it presents with pancytopenia due to bone marrow dysfunction. (Book p241)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-038"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "Odd one out (clinical features of hereditary spherocytosis listed in the book):", "opts": ["Neonatal jaundice", "Splenomegaly", "Pigment gallstones", "Dactylitis"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Dactylitis/hand-and-foot syndrome belongs to sickle cell anaemia, not hereditary spherocytosis. (Book p241)", "type": "odd", "id": "PEDS-C51-039"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The most likely precipitating event is —", "opts": ["Parvovirus B19-induced aplastic crisis", "Acute cholecystitis from pigment stones", "Superimposed iron deficiency", "Splenic sequestration crisis"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Parvovirus B19 in hereditary spherocytosis → bone marrow dysfunction → pancytopenia with reticulocytopenia. (Book p241)", "type": "case", "stem": "A 9-year-old girl with known hereditary spherocytosis develops fever and marked pallor; Hb falls from 9 to 4 g/dl with a reticulocyte count of 0.1% and low WBC and platelet counts.", "id": "PEDS-C51-040"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — clinical features · p241", "page": 241, "q": "The stones in this child are most likely —", "opts": ["Cholesterol stones from an obese diet", "Pigment stones from increased bilirubin production", "Struvite stones from urinary infection", "Calcium oxalate stones from hyperoxaluria"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Chronic haemolysis in hereditary spherocytosis produces pigment gallstones. (Book p241)", "type": "case", "stem": "A 5-year-old with chronic anaemia and jaundice presents with right upper quadrant pain; ultrasound shows multiple small echogenic stones in the gallbladder.", "id": "PEDS-C51-041"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "The peripheral smear hallmark of a spherocyte is —", "opts": ["Basophilic stippling", "A central pallor exceeding one-third of the cell", "Lack of central pallor", "A target-shaped central density"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Peripheral smear: lack of central pallor in spherocytes. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-042"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "During the shape change in hereditary spherocytosis a small ___ of the RBC is lost, starting the sequence that ends in hyperchromia.", "opts": ["Volume of cytoplasm", "Quantity of membrane iron", "Amount of haemoglobin", "Surface area"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Small surface area of RBC is lost during shape change → cellular dehydration → ↑ Hb concentration → hyperchromia. (Book p242)", "type": "fill", "id": "PEDS-C51-043"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Loss of surface area during the shape change leads first to —", "opts": ["Cellular dehydration", "Cellular swelling", "Nuclear extrusion", "Heinz body formation"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Loss of surface area → cellular dehydration. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-044"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Cellular dehydration of the spherocyte raises the intracellular haemoglobin concentration and thereby causes —", "opts": ["Polychromasia", "Hyperchromia", "Anisocytosis", "Hypochromia"], "ans": 1, "exp": "↑ concentration of Hb inside the RBC → hyperchromia. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-045"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "The red cell index described as characteristic of hereditary spherocytosis is —", "opts": ["Increased RDW alone", "Increased MCV", "Increased MCHC", "Decreased MCH"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Red cell indices: ↑ MCHC (characteristic). (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-046"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "True or False — MCHC is characteristically increased in hereditary spherocytosis.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 0, "exp": "True — ↑ MCHC is the characteristic red cell index. (Book p242)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-047"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Osmotic fragility in hereditary spherocytosis is —", "opts": ["Increased", "Unchanged", "Increased only after splenectomy", "Decreased"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Screening tests: osmotic fragility — increased. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-048"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Increased osmotic fragility begins with increased permeability of spherocytes to ___.", "opts": ["Potassium", "Sodium", "Calcium", "Chloride"], "ans": 1, "exp": "↑ permeability to Na+ in spherocytes → ↑ Na+/H2O transport into RBC. (Book p242)", "type": "fill", "id": "PEDS-C51-049"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Increased Na+/H2O transport into the spherocyte results in —", "opts": ["Lysis only in hypertonic saline solutions", "Crenation of the cell without any lysis", "Swelling and lysis at lower hypotonic saline concentrations", "Resistance to lysis even in distilled water"], "ans": 2, "exp": "↑ Na+/H2O entry → ↑ swelling & lysis at lower concentration of hypotonic saline. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-050"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "In the incubated osmotic fragility test, the patient's blood is incubated at —", "opts": ["4°C for 24 hours", "37°C for 1 hour", "25°C for 6 hours", "37°C for 24 hours"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Incubated osmotic fragility: patient's blood incubated at 37°C for 24 hours. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-051"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "The stated purpose of the incubated osmotic fragility test is to —", "opts": ["Improve the sensitivity of testing", "Quantify the MCHC", "Detect Heinz bodies", "Confirm the diagnosis genetically"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Incubated osmotic fragility improves sensitivity of testing. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-052"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "True or False — Increased osmotic fragility is specific to hereditary spherocytosis.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 1, "exp": "False — the book notes that osmotic fragility is seen in other haemolytic disorders as well. (Book p242)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-053"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Which test is listed as confirmatory for hereditary spherocytosis?", "opts": ["MCHC estimation", "Incubated osmotic fragility", "Flow cytometry for eosin-5-maleimide binding", "Peripheral smear for spherocytes"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Confirmatory tests: flow cytometry (EMA binding) and molecular testing. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-054"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Flow cytometry in hereditary spherocytosis shows ___ binding of eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) to red cell proteins.", "opts": ["Fluctuating", "Increased", "Unchanged", "Decreased"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Flow cytometry: ↓ binding of eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) to proteins. (Book p242)", "type": "fill", "id": "PEDS-C51-055"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "The dye used in the confirmatory flow cytometric test for hereditary spherocytosis is —", "opts": ["Eosin-5-maleimide", "Sodium metabisulphite", "Prussian blue", "Brilliant cresyl blue"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Flow cytometry uses eosin-5-maleimide (EMA). (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-056"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Molecular testing in hereditary spherocytosis looks for —", "opts": ["Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies", "Deletion of mutations in RBCs", "HbS quantification", "Enzyme levels in RBCs"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Molecular testing: deletion of mutations in RBCs. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-057"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Match the investigation in hereditary spherocytosis with its finding/role:", "opts": ["A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4", "A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3", "A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2", "A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Smear and indices describe the cell; osmotic fragility screens; flow cytometry confirms. Correct pairs: Peripheral smear→Lack of central pallor in spherocytes; Red cell indices→Increased MCHC — characteristic; Osmotic fragility→Screening test, also positive in other haemolytic disorders; Flow cytometry→Confirmatory — reduced EMA binding. (Book p242)", "type": "match", "pairs": [["Peripheral smear", "Increased MCHC — characteristic"], ["Red cell indices", "Confirmatory — reduced EMA binding"], ["Osmotic fragility", "Lack of central pallor in spherocytes"], ["Flow cytometry", "Screening test, also positive in other haemolytic disorders"]], "id": "PEDS-C51-058"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Odd one out (screening tests in hereditary spherocytosis):", "opts": ["Peripheral smear morphology", "Osmotic fragility", "Incubated osmotic fragility", "Flow cytometry for EMA binding"], "ans": 3, "exp": "EMA flow cytometry is a confirmatory test, not a screening test. (Book p242)", "type": "odd", "id": "PEDS-C51-059"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "Which test will confirm the suspected diagnosis?", "opts": ["Flow cytometry for eosin-5-maleimide binding", "G6PD enzyme assay in red cells", "Haemoglobin electrophoresis", "Incubated osmotic fragility at 37°C"], "ans": 0, "exp": "Screening points to hereditary spherocytosis; the confirmatory test is EMA flow cytometry. (Book p242)", "type": "case", "stem": "A 4-year-old with anaemia, jaundice and splenomegaly has a smear showing red cells without central pallor, an MCHC of 37 g/dl and increased osmotic fragility.", "id": "PEDS-C51-060"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "The most appropriate next step described in the book is —", "opts": ["Transfuse and repeat the test after 3 months", "Repeat after incubating the blood at 37°C for 24 hours", "Declare the diagnosis excluded on this result", "Proceed directly to a laparoscopic splenectomy"], "ans": 1, "exp": "Incubated osmotic fragility (37°C for 24 h) improves the sensitivity of testing. (Book p242)", "type": "case", "stem": "A child with suspected hereditary spherocytosis has a borderline standard osmotic fragility result.", "id": "PEDS-C51-061"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — investigations · p242", "page": 242, "q": "True or False — Hyperchromia of the spherocyte is explained by cellular dehydration raising the intracellular haemoglobin concentration.", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": 0, "exp": "True — loss of surface area → cellular dehydration → ↑ Hb concentration inside the RBC → hyperchromia. (Book p242)", "type": "tf", "id": "PEDS-C51-062"},
 {"sec": "Hereditary spherocytosis — management and splenectomy · p242–243", "page": 242, "q": "Folic acid supplementation in hereditary spherocytosis is given —", "opts": ["Only after splenectomy", "For 6 months only", "During crises alone", "Lifelong"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Management: lifelong folic acid supplementation. (Book p242)", "type": "mcq", "id": "PEDS-C51-063"},
